    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>Syst??????me de cote ??????nerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="9" build="20154">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.9b20154</English>
                <French>v15.9b20154</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16" build="20154">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16b20154</English>
                <French>v1.16b20154</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="3" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="3" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, I identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around your basement header, particularly between the joists above the washer and dryer. 



** Sliding windows that do not seal well between panes of glass.



** The current 5" chimney and 5" combustion air supply pipe for the furnace and hot water tank, which can be eliminated by replacing these with direct vent appliances.



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $490 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 18.0 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation. I recommend you add a minimum R15 insulation to these exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding.



The new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019 can help you cover the cost of the additional insulation. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added to the above foundation exterior walls ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings: By adding a minimum R15 to all of the above foundation exterior walls, you could potentially save $590 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 21.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $5,000 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



If you are planning on completing a siding replacement before January 31st 2022, please contact me and we will discuss your next steps for participation in this program. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

As there is no interior access to your attic, I have assumed it to have a total of R30 of insulation. I have modeled the energy savings if you were to increase the attic insulation up to a minimum R50. Please be sure you maintain adequate attic ventilation and do not block any soffit venting.



Savings: By increasing all of the attic insulation from R30 up to a minimum R50, this is estimated to save $80 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.9 GJ, and qualify for a $125 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</ceilings>
                <windows>

While most of the windows in your house are already triple pane, most of them are sliding windows and most do not appear to have a low-E coating. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing your older sliding windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $85 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.1 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 150 litre propane hot water tank with a continuous pilot light. You may want to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $105 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.2 GJ,  and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 79.2% efficient propane furnace, with a continuous pilot light. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



This would eliminate the continuous pilot light, as well as your combustion air duct that brings air in for your furnace and hot water tank. 



Savings: By replacing your current propane furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $2,300 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 86.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. In addition, LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 4.7 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and your wood stove vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see smoke detectors but no carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - a range hood   

        - three bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that seals well, uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife

The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.21 per liter 

Propane - $0.683 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,

Alexandra Giroux, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: alexandra.giroux@aea.nt.ca

Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
